. San Antonio police have publicly said they will enforce the state classification, and the same statewide law applies to other Texas jurisdictions."]}, {"heading": "What people are watching", "paragraphs": ["Retailers now have strong incentives to remove affected inventory, check supplier testing and verify the cannabinoid profile of every ingestible or inhalable hemp product. Businesses that relied heavily on delta-8 or similar products may have to absorb losses or shift to products that fit the remaining legal framework."]}, {"heading": "What comes next", "paragraphs": ["Texans should rely on current state guidance and qualified legal advice rather than old social-media posts or store clerks when determining whether a product is lawful. Hemp rules have changed repeatedly, and the consequences of getting the answer wrong can be far more serious than a simple regulatory citation."]}, {"heading": "Why it matters now", "paragraphs": ["The enforcement shift is likely to create continued confusion because products sold under the broad label of hemp can contain very different cannabinoids and concentrations. Consumers should not assume that an item is lawful simply because it remains on a store shelf or was purchased before police agencies changed their guidance. Retailers, meanwhile, face the risk that inventory considered marketable under earlier interpretations may now create criminal exposure. The legal landscape could still change through legislation or additional court action, but for now the practical question is how consistently local agencies apply the state’s controlled-substance rules across thousands of shops and products. As this story develops, readers should distinguish confirmed details from social-media speculation and watch the original reporting and official records for material updates. Keep TX Red will treat later changes as updates rather than silently folding unverified claims into the record."]}], "faq": [], "sources": [{"label": "San Antonio Exp